{
  "data": {
    "text": "General, crystallized and fluid intelligence are not associated with functional global network efficiency: A replication study with the human connectome project 1200 data set\n\nJ.D. Kruschwitz, L. Waller, L.S. Daedelow, H. Walter, I.M. Veer\n\nNeuroImage 171 (2018) 323–331 — 1053-8119/© 2018 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2018.01.018\n\nWe were unable to provide evidence for the proposed association between general intelligence and functional brain network efficiency, or for any other association with the global network measures employed.\n\nAll 1096 participants with resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging from the Human Connectome Project (HCP) 1200 subjects release were entered into the analysis. As a result, sample size varied between 999 and 1096.\n\nGender (male/female) 500/596; Age 28.8 ± 3.7 years; Handedness 66.0 ± 44.0; Mean framewise displacement (FD) 0.09 ± 0.03 mm.\n\nCrystallized cognition (Picture vocabulary, Oral reading) and Fluid cognition (Dimensional card sort, Flanker, Picture sequence memory, List sorting, Processing speed) were aggregated to a Total Cognition Score; fluid intelligence was also measured with Raven’s Progressive Matrices.\n\nSubjects were scanned in four 15min runs at 2mm isotropic resolution and a repetition time of 0.72s in a 3T Siemens connectome-Skyra scanner (4800 vol per subject). Scans were processed according to the HCP minimal preprocessing pipeline, which includes B0-unwarping and normalization to the MNI-152 template. A second pipeline, the netmats pipeline, removed artifacts using FMRIB's ICA-based X-noisifier (ICA+FIX).\n\nGroup-ICA data was performed using MELODIC's incremental group PCA and subject-specific time series were derived using dual regression. Using these time series, connectivity matrices were derived using the FSLNets toolbox, either via full correlation or partial correlation with L2-regularization with a regularization parameter of 0.01, the latter being calculated by inverting the covariance matrix.\n\nNetworks were defined using voxel-wise data and parcellations: automated anatomical labeling (AAL, 116 regions), Power et al. (264 regions), Craddock et al. (840 regions), and 100, 200, and 300-component HCP group-ICA solutions.\n\nThese networks were thresholded using a proportional threshold in the range of 0.1–0.5 in steps of 0.01.\n\nFor each network we computed global efficiency, characteristic path length, and global clustering coefficient. All analyses were performed with GraphVar (version 1.03 ‘Turbo-GLM’) and an optimized voxel-wise program (https://github.com/HippocampusGirl/Massive)."
  },
